-- title:
--   Finite p-torsion splits off the two degeneracy pull-backs
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, a positive integer $M$ with $p \mid M$ but $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$, together with the hypothesis that the $q$-expansion $j(q)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the function field of the full modular group. Given an integral model datum $\mathfrak{X}$ of $X_H(M)$ over $R_p = \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, level data $\Lambda$ at level $M/p$ (a scheme $\Lambda.X \to \operatorname{Spec} R_p$ with a relative group law and bijections of $J_{H'}(M/p)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the mod-$p$ level-$(M/p)$ function field with the generic, resp. residual, sections), and an object $O$ consisting of a smooth separated $G \to \operatorname{Spec} R_p$ with commutative relative group law, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts} : J_H(M)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) \to$ generic sections, and Hecke data, assume: $G$ together with the unit section represents the rigidified fibrewise-algebraically-trivial relative Picard condition over $R_p$ (hypothesis `hD`) and likewise after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ (`hDQ`), this base change being separated; an Abel–Jacobi morphism $ajQ$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and a comparison morphism $kQ$ between the fibres over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and over $\mathbb{Q}$, with the Poincaré bundle compatibility `hpoinc`, the normalisation `hajQε` sending the cusp section to the zero section, the Abel–Jacobi property `hajQ` identifying the pull-back of the Poincaré bundle along a point $x$ with the tensor product of the line bundle of the divisor of $x$ and the ideal module of the divisor of the base point, and the pull-back identities `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`; the induced geometric Abel–Jacobi map $\overline{aj}$ given by `hajbar`, lying over the generic point, a base point $\bar\varepsilon$ with `hεbar` and $\bar\varepsilon \circ \overline{aj}$ the unit, additivity of $O.\mathrm{pts}$ for the relative group law induced by the representability, and the Abel–Jacobi description `hAJ`: for all geometric points $x$ and $s$ with $s$ the cusp, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[x] - [s]$ whose class maps under $O.\mathrm{pts}$ to $x$ followed by $\overline{aj}$. Assume further that $\Lambda.f$ satisfies the abelian-scheme property bundle over $R_p$ (smooth, proper, connected fibres, a relative group law), that $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ and $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ are additive; let $F$ be the additive endomorphism of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the mod-$p$ function field given by the $q$-expansion Frobenius push-forward, $F^{-1}$ a two-sided inverse of it, $F^\ast = p\,F^{-1}$, $\delta$ the action of the diamond operator attached to a unit $\bar p$ of $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ reducing to $p$; and let $\alpha^\ast_0, \alpha^\ast_1 : J_{H'}(M/p)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) \to J_H(M)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ be additive maps realised by morphisms $\mathrm{degPull}_i : \Lambda.X \to G$ over the base which are compatible with the group laws and satisfy, on residual sections, $z \mapsto (z, F^\ast z)$ for $i = 0$ and $z \mapsto (F^\ast z, \delta z)$ for $i = 1$ in the glued $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ coordinates. The conclusion: every $y$ in the subgroup $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$ generated by the $p$-torsion classes whose associated section extends to an $A$-point can be written $y = y_0 + \alpha^\ast_0 u + \alpha^\ast_1 v$ with $p u = p v = 0$, and with $y_0$ admitting a section $s$ of $G$ over $A$ such that $O.\mathrm{pts}(y_0)$ is $\bar{\cdot}$ composed with $s$ and the reduction of $s$ is the unit section.
--
--   This is the decomposition of the finite $p$-torsion of the Néron object attached to $J_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing $M$ into a part with trivial reduction and the images of the two degeneracy pull-backs from level $M/p$; it plays the role of Grothendieck's isotropy computation in the multiplicative-reduction analysis. It is used in the verification that the Weil pairing between toric and finite $p$-torsion classes is trivial, a step of the level-lowering argument.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP_exists_eq_add_pull_add_pull_of_mem_finPts_of_abelJacobiPin.lean

